\section{Analysis \& Discussion}
\label{sec:analysis}

The preceding results establish that trained $\dt$-embedding is the causal factor behind pathological value dynamics in variable-rate DreamerV3. In this section, we synthesize these findings into a mechanistic account of conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO), explain why the same CIVO trigger produces divergent outcomes across seeds, contrast the imagination-based setting with MPC-based temporal conditioning, and discuss the fundamental tension inherent in the $\dt$-embedding design.

\subsection{The CIVO Mechanism}
\label{sec:civo_mechanism}

The RSSM fidelity analysis (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}) reveals the mechanistic core of CIVO.  The trained $\dt$-embedding enables the reward head to learn temporally differentiated predictions, but these predictions are asymmetrically accurate: reward MAE at $\dt{=}1$ is $0.0568$, while at $\dt{=}2$ it rises to $0.1579$---a $2.78\times$ gap.  This gap is not an artifact of prediction difficulty alone; the no-embedding configuration (008f) exhibits a $1.01\times$ gap, and the baseline shows $1.56\times$.  The trained embedding actively amplifies the fidelity discrepancy by allowing the reward head to co-adapt with the temporal signal.

This asymmetric fidelity initiates a feedback loop with four stages: (1) the trained $\dt$-embedding causes RSSM predictions at $\dt > 1$ to carry systematically biased reward estimates; (2) the critic, trained entirely on imagined trajectories, internalizes these biased rewards as optimistic value estimates; (3) the actor exploits the overestimated regions of the value landscape, shifting its policy toward states that appear high-value only under the biased model; and (4) this policy shift further skews the distribution of imagined states, reinforcing the bias.  The diagnostic signature is a sustained $\nve$ above $1.5\times$, followed by evaluation collapse.  In exp\_008d (seed 0), $\nve$ rises from $1.78\times$ at 25K to $4.12\times$ at 55K while evaluation falls from 167 to 82.8---a value-performance divergence of $+130\%$ in imagined value against $-50\%$ in realized return.

This mechanism is analogous to the delusional bias identified by \citet{lu2018nondelusional}, but differs in origin.  In the original formulation, delusional bias arises because the critic assigns value to states unreachable by the constrained policy class; the bias is structural, residing in the mismatch between policy expressivity and value approximation.  In CIVO, the critic assigns overestimated values because the reward predictions at $\dt > 1$ are systematically biased \emph{upward} by the learned temporal conditioning---the bias is model-induced, arising from the interaction between a learned input feature (the embedding) and the reward head's co-adaptation to it.  The consequence is similar---policy exploitation of overestimated regions---but the causal pathway is distinct: model imprecision amplified by learned conditioning, rather than policy-class constraints.

\subsection{Three Post-CIVO Outcomes: Why Seeds Diverge}
\label{sec:seed_divergence}

Running the identical 008d configuration (trained $\dt$-embedding, subsampling ON, $H{=}8$) across three seeds produces three qualitatively distinct outcomes (Table~\ref{tab:post_civo}).  Seed 0 exhibits full collapse: $\nve$ sustains above $1.5\times$ from 25K onward, value rises $+130\%$, and evaluation declines monotonically to 82.8.  Seed 2 self-corrects: $\nve$ transiently peaks at $2.05\times$ at 25K but the value function recalibrates to $0.38\times$ by 105K, with evaluation recovering to 267.9.  Seed 1 shows oscillatory degradation: evaluation swings from 82.7 (55K) to 250 (65K) to 178 (75K), while the value function monotonically rises from 138 to 190, producing a fluctuating $\nve$ trajectory.

All three seeds cross the $\nve > 1.5\times$ threshold---CIVO triggers deterministically---yet the downstream damage is stochastic.  We hypothesize that the divergence relates to the critic's representational capacity at the time of CIVO onset.  \citet{lyle2022capacity} demonstrate that deep RL networks suffer progressive capacity loss during training, in which early-learned features become increasingly difficult to overwrite.  If the critic's capacity is already partially consumed by the time CIVO injects overestimation, the network cannot recalibrate, producing the permanent collapse of seed 0.  If sufficient capacity remains, the critic can absorb the transient overestimation and recover, as in seed 2.

The oscillatory pattern of seed 1 is particularly informative.  The value function rises monotonically even as evaluation oscillates, indicating that the critic has lost calibration with respect to the true value landscape---it can no longer track realized returns.  The evaluation oscillation itself likely reflects the actor alternating between exploitation of overestimated regions and exploration driven by entropy regularization.  Unlike seed 0's monotonic decline (entropy decreasing), seed 1's entropy remains elevated, suggesting the actor has not fully committed to the overestimated policy but cannot escape it either.  This intermediate state---persistent overestimation without convergent collapse---may represent a regime in which partial capacity loss allows the critic to respond to new data without being able to fully correct its biases.

\subsection{Why MPC Is Immune}
\label{sec:mpc_immune}

\citet{nhu2025tawm} demonstrate that $\dt$-conditioning is beneficial in the MPC framework of TD-MPC2, raising the question of why the same technique fails in DreamerV3.  The critical architectural difference lies in how the value function interacts with the world model.  In MPC, the agent re-plans from real states at every environment step, using the world model only for short-horizon action selection via sampling-based optimization.  Imagination errors do not accumulate into the training signal because there is no critic bootstrapping from imagined trajectories---the value function, if used at all, serves as a terminal cost estimate and is trained on real transitions.  In DreamerV3, by contrast, the actor-critic is trained \emph{entirely} within imagination.  The critic's value estimates feed back into actor training across full $H$-step rollouts, and any systematic reward-prediction bias at $\dt > 1$ is amplified through this closed loop.  The feedback architecture---not the temporal conditioning itself---is what transforms a manageable model imprecision into a pathological training signal.  This distinction aligns with \citet{frauenknecht2025rollouts}, who find that rollout quality is a dominant factor in model-based RL performance, and with the time-discretization analysis of \citet{tallec2019making}, who show that temporal resolution mismatches can destabilize value learning in model-free settings.

\subsection{The $\dt$-Embedding Dilemma}
\label{sec:embedding_dilemma}

The factorial results reveal a fundamental tension in the design of temporal conditioning for imagination-based RL.  Removing the $\dt$-embedding entirely (008f) eliminates CIVO: $\nve$ remains at $0.30\times$ with no collapse.  But this comes at a severe cost to world-model quality: reward prediction MAE rises to $0.7469$---a $13\times$ degradation relative to 008d's $0.0568$ at $\dt{=}1$.  Without the embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish temporal scales, producing uniformly poor predictions regardless of $\dt$.  The world model is effectively blind to temporal resolution: it can neither exploit nor be exploited by the temporal signal.

The mitigation experiment (008e\_v3) suggests a partial compromise: retain the trained $\dt$-embedding for observation conditioning but restrict imagination to $\dt{=}1$ exclusively.  This prevents CIVO (Pathway A) in 2 of 3 seeds, with seed 1 achieving 869.9 at 105K ($95\%$ of baseline).  However, seed 2 collapses to 137.0 via a qualitatively different mechanism (Pathway B), demonstrating that the trained embedding creates vulnerability even when $\dt > 1$ imagination is disabled.  Neither removing the embedding nor restricting imagination alone provides a complete solution.

\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}
\label{sec:pathways}

The partial falsification of C6 (that restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ prevents collapse) reveals that the trained $\dt$-embedding creates vulnerability through at least two mechanistically distinct pathways.

\textbf{Pathway A (CIVO, value-driven).}  Trained $\dt$-embedding $\rightarrow$ RSSM produces asymmetrically biased predictions at $\dt > 1$ $\rightarrow$ reward head systematic error $\rightarrow$ critic overestimation ($\nve > 1.5\times$) $\rightarrow$ actor exploits overestimated regions $\rightarrow$ performance collapse.  This pathway requires $\dt > 1$ imagination and produces a characteristic signature: value explosion preceding evaluation decline, with entropy \emph{decreasing} as the actor concentrates on overestimated regions.  Exp\_008d seed 0 is the canonical example.

\textbf{Pathway B (non-CIVO, eval-driven).}  Trained $\dt$-embedding $\rightarrow$ policy instability $\rightarrow$ evaluation crashes ($-81\%$) while the value function remains initially stable $\rightarrow$ a late $\nve$ spike emerges as a consequence of the eval decline, not a cause.  This pathway does \emph{not} require $\dt > 1$ imagination: exp\_008e\_v3 seed 2 imagines exclusively at $\dt{=}1$.  The signature is distinct from Pathway A: entropy \emph{increases} ($-5.21 \rightarrow -3.83$), indicating the actor is becoming more stochastic rather than less; the value function remains well-calibrated until evaluation has already degraded substantially.  The baseline with the same seed (009a) is stable at 836.5, ruling out inherent seed fragility.

The existence of two pathways broadens the diagnostic beyond CIVO alone.  The architectural coupling between the trained temporal embedding and the world model creates a more general vulnerability: the embedding's learned features affect not only reward prediction across temporal scales (Pathway A) but also the representation space in which the actor operates (Pathway B).  This suggests that any mitigation strategy focused exclusively on the imagination channel---restricting $\dt$, truncating rollouts, or smoothing rewards \citep{lee2024dreamsmooth}---may be insufficient.

\subsection{Implications for Temporal Abstraction in World Models}
\label{sec:implications}

The CIVO mechanism identifies a structural incompatibility between flat temporal conditioning and imagination-based actor-critic training.  The RSSM, designed for single-scale dynamics, cannot maintain prediction fidelity across temporal resolutions when conditioned via a learned embedding---the co-adaptation between embedding and reward head produces the asymmetric errors that drive the feedback loop.  Hierarchical approaches that abstract temporal scales at the representation level, such as THICK \citep{gumbsch2024thick}, Hieros \citep{mattes2024hieros}, and LS-Imagine \citep{li2025lsimagine}, may avoid this failure mode by maintaining separate dynamics models for each temporal scale rather than conditioning a single model on $\dt$.

DreamerV4 \citep{hafner2025dreamerv4} replaces the RSSM with a transformer architecture, raising the question of whether CIVO transfers to attention-based world models.  The transformer's capacity for in-context temporal reasoning could either mitigate the asymmetric fidelity problem---by better modeling multi-scale dynamics within a single architecture---or amplify it, if attention weights co-adapt with the temporal embedding in a manner analogous to the RSSM's reward head.  Resolving this question requires extending the present diagnostic framework to transformer-based world models.

More broadly, regularization of the $\dt$-embedding (\eg, gradient stopping to prevent co-adaptation with the reward head, or spectral normalization on the embedding weights) could potentially preserve the temporal signal's benefits for world-model training while preventing the asymmetric reward-prediction bias that initiates CIVO.  We leave empirical evaluation of such approaches to future work.

