\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Temporal Abstraction in Model-Free RL.}
Temporal abstraction has been a central theme in reinforcement learning since the options framework \citep{sutton1999options}, which formalized multi-step decision-making via temporally extended actions over semi-MDPs. Subsequent work has explored learned action repetition: FiGAR \citep{sharma2017figar} augments the action space with a learned repeat duration, TempoRL \citep{biedenkapp2021temporl} learns when to act via a skip-policy, and SDAR \citep{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic} selects per-dimension repetition rates. These methods demonstrate substantial speedups in model-free settings, where the value function is trained on real transitions and is thus insulated from model-induced bias. Our work investigates whether analogous temporal abstraction can be achieved within imagination-based (model-based) RL, and identifies a failure mode specific to the latter.

\paragraph{World-Model RL and Imagination.}
The Dreamer family \citep{hafner2020dreamerv1, hafner2021dreamerv2, hafner2023dreamerv3} trains actor-critic policies entirely within imagined trajectories generated by a learned world model (the RSSM). Alternative architectures include IRIS \citep{micheli2023iris} and TWM \citep{robine2023twm}, which replace the RSSM with autoregressive transformers. Most recently, DreamerV4 \citep{hafner2025dreamerv4} replaces the RSSM with a transformer world model and introduces shortcut forcing for scalable video pretraining. All of these systems operate at a single temporal resolution during imagination ($\dt{=}1$). Our diagnostic examines what happens when this constraint is relaxed---and why it fails.

\paragraph{Variable-Rate World Models.}
Several recent works address temporal abstraction within world models, but via fundamentally different mechanisms than the flat $\dt$-conditioning we diagnose. THICK \citep{gumbsch2024thick} learns hierarchical temporal abstractions in Dreamer by detecting context changes in discrete latent dynamics, abstracting temporal scales at the representation level rather than conditioning a single model on $\dt$. Hieros \citep{mattes2024hieros} extends this idea with multi-scale hierarchical imagination over structured state-space models. LS-Imagine \citep{li2025lsimagine} extends imagination horizons via goal-conditioned jumpy state transitions and affordance maps, avoiding $\dt$-embedding entirely. MTS3 \citep{shaj2023mts3} provides a multi-time-scale state-space model for variable-rate prediction, but focuses on prediction quality rather than downstream policy training. DreamSmooth \citep{lee2024dreamsmooth} and HarmonyDream \citep{ma2024harmonydream} address reward-prediction quality in world models---related to our finding that reward-head fidelity gates value estimation, but orthogonal to the temporal conditioning mechanism.

The most directly relevant concurrent work is TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm}, which explicitly conditions a world model on time-step size $\Delta t$ and demonstrates that this conditioning improves performance in TD-MPC2 \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}. The critical distinction is architectural: TAWM operates within an MPC framework, where the agent re-plans from real states at every environment step and model errors do not accumulate into the critic's training signal. Our work shows that the same $\dt$-conditioning technique fails in imagination-based actor-critic training, where the critic bootstraps entirely from imagined trajectories---transforming manageable model imprecision into pathological value overestimation (CIVO). This MPC-vs-imagination contrast is central to our contribution: $\dt$-conditioning succeeds when errors are contained by re-planning but fails when errors compound through closed-loop imagination.

\paragraph{Value Overestimation in Model-Based RL.}
Value overestimation has been extensively studied in model-free settings \citep{fujimoto2018td3, vanhasselt2016double} and in model-based RL through the lens of model exploitation. MBPO \citep{janner2019mbpo} formalizes the risk of policy optimization under imperfect models, while MOPO \citep{yu2020mopo} introduces pessimistic reward penalties to mitigate exploitation in offline MBRL. \citet{lu2018nondelusional} identify delusional bias---value overestimation arising from policy-class constraints---and \citet{su2020conqur} propose consistency-penalized targets to mitigate it. Our CIVO mechanism is analogous but mechanistically distinct: delusional bias originates in the mismatch between policy expressivity and value approximation, whereas CIVO originates in model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning. \citet{tallec2019making} provide theoretical foundations for time-discretization sensitivity in value learning, showing that Q-function performance degrades with temporal resolution mismatches---a result that underpins our finding that RSSM predictions at $\dt > 1$ introduce systematic bias. \citet{frauenknecht2025rollouts} demonstrate that rollout quality is a dominant factor in model-based RL, with trajectory-sampling rollouts diverging from ground truth after few steps; our work identifies a specific mechanism---$\dt$-embedding-induced reward bias---by which this divergence corrupts value estimates. MAD-TD \citep{voelcker2025madtd} addresses value misgeneralization from distribution mismatch at high update ratios, a complementary failure mode to our temporal-conditioning-specific overestimation.

\paragraph{Diagnostic Methodology.}
Our work follows the tradition of systematic diagnostic studies in deep RL. \citet{engstrom2020implementation} demonstrate that implementation-level details, not algorithmic innovations, drive much of PPO's advantage over TRPO. \citet{andrychowicz2021whatmatters} conduct a large-scale factorial study of design choices in on-policy actor-critic methods. We adopt a similar factorial approach---isolating $\dt$-embedding training, temporal subsampling, and imagination horizon---to identify the specific architectural choice responsible for collapse in variable-rate imagination.

