\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We have presented a systematic factorial diagnostic of temporal abstraction in DreamerV3, identifying trained $\dt$-embedding as the causal factor behind conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO). The key findings are:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item Every variable-rate imagination configuration degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline, with degradation scaling monotonically with $\dt$.
\item Trained $\dt$-embedding reliably triggers pathological value overestimation ($\nve > 1.5\times$, 4/4 separating power), while untrained or removed embeddings prevent it.
\item The RSSM reward head develops a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning, providing the systematic bias that drives critic overestimation.
\item The same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes---full collapse, self-correction, and oscillatory degradation---across seeds of identical configurations.
\item Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ does not fully prevent collapse: one of two seeds fails via a qualitatively distinct non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven with increasing entropy), demonstrating that the trained $\dt$-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple mechanisms.
\end{enumerate}

The contrast with TAWM's success in MPC identifies the feedback architecture as the critical factor: $\dt$-conditioning succeeds when errors are contained by re-planning from real states, but fails when errors compound through imagination-based actor-critic bootstrapping.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our experiments focus on a single environment (Walker Walk) and a single world-model architecture (DreamerV3/RSSM). While multi-seed replication establishes robustness within this setting, generalization to other environments and architectures---particularly transformer-based world models such as DreamerV4---remains an open question. The nve diagnostic threshold ($1.5\times$) is empirically determined and may require recalibration for different tasks.

\paragraph{Future directions.}
Our findings suggest three avenues. First, hierarchical temporal abstraction at the representation level---as in THICK and Hieros---may avoid the flat-conditioning failure mode by maintaining separate dynamics for each temporal scale. Second, regularization of the $\dt$-embedding (\eg, gradient stopping or spectral normalization) could potentially preserve the temporal signal while preventing co-adaptation with the reward head. Third, extending this diagnostic framework to transformer-based world models and continuous-control benchmarks would clarify whether CIVO is RSSM-specific or a broader vulnerability of temporal conditioning in imagination-based RL.

\begin{abstract}
Temporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination is a natural extension of world-model reinforcement learning, yet we find that every variable-rate configuration of DreamerV3 degrades performance---despite the success of identical $\Delta t$-conditioning in MPC-based methods. Through a factorial diagnostic on Walker Walk, we identify trained temporal embedding as the causal factor and characterize the resulting failure mode: \emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). The trained embedding enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\Delta t{=}1$ and $\Delta t{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic values. A normalized value-to-eval ratio threshold of $1.5\times$ achieves 4/4 separating power between trained and untrained embedding configurations. The same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes across seeds---full collapse, self-correction, and oscillatory degradation---from identical configurations. Critically, restricting imagination to $\Delta t{=}1$ does not prevent collapse: one of two seeds fails via a distinct non-CIVO pathway with increasing entropy, revealing that trained temporal conditioning creates vulnerability through multiple mechanisms. Our analysis identifies the feedback architecture---imagination-based actor-critic bootstrapping versus MPC re-planning---as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics.
\end{abstract}

